1. First find the number of video games Brian has after he lost some. Brian had 20 games and lost 5, so he has 20 - 5 = 15 games. 2. Then determine the number of games Bobby has. According to the problem, Bobby has 5 fewer than 3 times as many games as Brian. Brian has 15 games, so 3 times that is 3 * 15 = 45. Then Bobby has 45 - 5 = 40 games.

Thus, Bobby has 40.
\boxed{40}

1. Count the total number of students in the school: 20 students/class * 3 classes = 60 students. 2. Calculate the number of boys in the school: 60 students * 50% = 30 boys. 3. Calculate the number of girls in the school: 60 students * 50% = 30 girls. 4. Calculate the number of girls in the first two classes: 15 girls + 12 girls = 27 girls. 5. Calculate the number of girls in the third class: 30 girls - 27 girls = 3 girls. 6. Calculate the number of boys in the third class: 30 boys - (number of boys in the first two classes) = ? First, find the number of boys in the first class: 20 students/class * 50% = 10 boys. Next, find the number of boys in the second class: 20 students/class * 50% = 10 boys. So, the number of boys in the first two classes is 10 + 10 = 20 boys. Then, the number of boys in the third class is 30 boys - 20 boys = 10 boys.

There are 10 boys in the third class.
\boxed{10}

